;;; ccr5-selfdual.scm -- SELF-DUALITY OF A LOCAL NV FIELD (CCR.pdf Proposition 5.13) BY A FINITE FOURIER
;;; DOUBLE SUM, and its corollaries 6.3 (converse), 6.6, 6.20, 6.23 for a local NV field.  Agent CCR-1109-D2,
;;; 2026-11-09.  Destination: theorem-library/ccr5-selfdual.scm, right after theorem-library/ccr5-dual.scm
;;; (it cites ccr-6-17-ball, ccr-6-18-unipolar, ccr-5-15, c5-lu-*, c5-polar-mem, c5-integers-mem and the four
;;; self-dual corollaries).  Library: complex-analysis (as ccr5-dual).  Helper prefix sdl-.
;;;
;;; THE ROUTE (no Haar measure, no Stone-Weierstrass).
;;;  * Cosets of a ball are balls (ultrametric).  CS(rs, rb) = { B(x, rs) : |x| <= rb } is the set of cosets of
;;;    B(rs) in B(rb); it is FINITE when K is local (B(rb) is compact, hence totally bounded).  A function g on K
;;;    that is constant on the cosets is summed over CS through the representative CHOICE(C).
;;;  * ORTHOGONALITY: if g is multiplicative, trivial on B(rs) and g(h) /= 1 for some |h| <= rb, then
;;;    sum_{C in CS} g(CHOICE C) = 0 -- the translation C -> B(CHOICE C + h, rs) is a bijection of CS
;;;    (inverse: translation by -h), which multiplies the sum by g(h).
;;;  * THE DOUBLE SUM.  phi is a character with phi = 1 on Z and, for |h| > 1, phi(h x) /= 1 for some x in Z
;;;    (the shape of a unipolar bicharacter, 6.13).  tau is a character trivial on B(|c|).  With
;;;    T = sum_{C in CS(|c|, |e|)} sum_{D in CS(|e^-1|, |c^-1|)} tau(rC) phi(-(rD) rC):
;;;      summing over D first gives T = card(CS(|e^-1|, |c^-1|)) /= 0 (orthogonality in D unless rC in B(|c|));
;;;      summing over C first gives T = 0 unless tau = phi(p .) on B(|e|) for some p = rD.
;;;    So tau agrees with a dilate of phi on every ball (Remark 5.14, finite form).
;;;  * THE LIMIT.  a_k with tau = phi(a_k .) on B(|uw|^-k) agree with a_0 on Z; Lemma 5.15 (ccr-5-15) gives a
;;;    convergent subsequence, and its limit a has tau = phi(a .) everywhere.
;;;  * 5.13: for a non-trivial chi, 6.18 gives a unipolar w; phi = w(1, .) has the two properties; chi = phi(a1 .),
;;;    a1 /= 0, so every tau = phi(a .) = chi((a / a1) .).

